  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool, quiet, and dry weather prevails through the \nfirst half of Sunday. Expect Highs in the 60s with a chilly start to \nSaturday morning, especially in the typical cold spots. \n\nA broad mid level trough slowly traverses overhead Friday as high \npressure ridging approaches from the west. This places SNE beneath a \nNW 30-35 kt LLJ; with good mixing (bufkit showing to near 850mb) \nexpecting a gusty day today, much like Thursday. Otherwise, \nbeautiful, mostly sunny weather expected with highs in the mid 60s \nand a mix of diurnal clouds. High pressure continues to expand and \nmove east, directly overhead by Saturday afternoon giving a very \nnice start to the holiday weekend with sun, light winds, and highs \nin the 60s. Something to watch will be how quickly that pressure \ngradient weakens because light/calm winds overnight would allow for \nefficient radiational cooling. Thus, leaned on MOS guidance for \nSaturday morning low temperatures. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next quick moving round of rain comes Sunday \nafternoon and evening, continuing overnight and exiting by Monday \nmorning. \n\nThe main change from 24 hours ago is both a slightly quicker arrival \nand exit of the rain. Guidance is in agreement that the moisture \nleft over from what used to be Hurricane Isaias will be pulled east \nby a northern stream trough and quickly ushered through the mid \nAtlantic and offshore. There is less agreement as to exactly how far \nnorth the precip shield is, but continuing to expect southern New \nEngland to be well within the area that will see rain. One one hand, \nparts of the area (especially west) likely have to deal with rain \nshowers Sunday afternoon/evening. 8PM Sunday ensemble guidance \nindicates a 80-90% chance of at least 0.01\" rain for western MA/CT, \nbut only 40-50% for far eastern MA. On the other hand, by Monday \nmorning there's a good agreement the rain has exited east so it \nshould be a mostly overnight event. On the whole this looks to be a \nfairly typical rainfall producing system for the region without \nhazards from flooding or damaging winds. Guidance generally paints a \npicture of a half inch for northern MA to 1+\" along the south shore \nin closer proximity to the better forcing and moisture. While EPS \nensemble probabilities of >1\" of rain have increased to max 40-50% \nalong the immediate south coast, GEFS probs put the odds at \nessentially NIL; needless to say, uncertainty remains on who will \nsee the most rain and how much. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Quick round of rain around mid week, then dry \nweather returns by Thursday.  \n\nA break in the rain comes Monday into Tuesday under brief high \npressure ridging before another frontal system moves through \nbringing a round of wet weather. Confidence in any timing or \nplacement details is low and will depend on how this holiday \nweekend's system plays out but unsettled weather looks possible \naround late Tuesday or Wednesday.",
